IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mc97-nouns`
IMPORT `mc97-saving`
IMPORT `mc97-seat`

-- MAGNA CARTA (1297) -- WORKED CASES FOR THE SEAT LAYER (mc97-seat.l4), one persona at least per question.
-- Each case states the facts a person in the seat could state, then asserts the answer. Every expected value is written from the text
-- (extract lines 23 to 47), before the run, and none is copied from the encoding's output. The inputs are positional, in the order of
-- the question's GIVEN lines in mc97-seat.l4.
-- The last section calls a layer-1 rule directly: the subsidy has no seat question (see the comment at the head of mc97-seat.l4).

§ `Worked cases for the seat`

§§ `The preamble: Am I among those to whom the Liberties following are given?`

-- An Earl asks. Preamble paragraph 2 names Earls.
#ASSERT `Am I among those to whom the Liberties following are given and granted` `an Earl` EQUALS `covered by the text`
-- A Freeman of the Realm asks. The word [Freemen] is bracketed in the text but it is in the text.
#ASSERT `Am I among those to whom the Liberties following are given and granted` `a Freeman of the Realm` EQUALS `covered by the text`
-- A Knight asks. Paragraph 2 does not name Knights: the answer is that the text is silent, not that the answer is no.
#ASSERT `Am I among those to whom the Liberties following are given and granted` `a Knight` EQUALS `not covered by the text`

§§ `Chapter 1: the Church of England, and the Freemen`

-- Someone asks about the Church of England: chapter 1 says it shall be free, and shall have all her whole Rights and Liberties inviolable.
#ASSERT `Is the Church of England free` TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Does the Church of England have all her whole Rights and Liberties inviolable` TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`
-- Someone asks about another body, say a guild: chapter 1 names only the Church of England.
#ASSERT `Is the Church of England free` FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Does the Church of England have all her whole Rights and Liberties inviolable` FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- A Freeman of the Realm: holds the Liberties under-written.
#ASSERT `Do I hold the Liberties under-written` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `holds as a Freeman of the Realm`
-- The Heir of a Freeman, who is not himself a Freeman: "to them and their Heirs".
#ASSERT `Do I hold the Liberties under-written` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `holds as an Heir of a Freeman of the Realm`
-- A traveller who is neither: the sentence is silent about them.
#ASSERT `Do I hold the Liberties under-written` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by chapter 1's second sentence`

§§ `Chapter 9: a City, a Borough, a Town, a Port`

-- The City of London asks what it shall have: all the old Liberties and Customs which it hath been used to have.
#ASSERT `What does chapter 9 say my community shall have` `the City of London` EQUALS `all the old Liberties and Customs which it hath been used to have`
-- A Borough asks: all their Liberties and free Customs.
#ASSERT `What does chapter 9 say my community shall have` `a Borough` EQUALS `all their Liberties and free Customs`
-- A Baron of the Five Ports asks: the same words.
#ASSERT `What does chapter 9 say my community shall have` `a Baron of the Five Ports` EQUALS `all their Liberties and free Customs`
-- A village asks (a community of another kind): chapter 9 does not name it.
#ASSERT `What does chapter 9 say my community shall have` `a community of another kind` EQUALS `not covered by chapter 9`
-- Arguments: the community; it is a Custom (not a Liberty); it is old; my community hath been used to have it; it is my community's own; the Custom is free.
-- The City asks about an old Custom it hath been used to have: kept.
#ASSERT `Does my community keep this Liberty or Custom` `the City of London` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `covered by the text`
-- The City asks about an old Liberty it has not been used to have: the bracketed words are not met, so the text is silent.
#ASSERT `Does my community keep this Liberty or Custom` `the City of London` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- A Borough asks about its own free Custom: kept.
#ASSERT `Does my community keep this Liberty or Custom` `a Borough` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`
-- A Borough asks about its own Custom that is not free: not kept by this sentence.
#ASSERT `Does my community keep this Liberty or Custom` `a Borough` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- A Town asks about its own Liberty (no word "free" is asked of a Liberty): kept.
#ASSERT `Does my community keep this Liberty or Custom` `a Town` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `covered by the text`
-- A Town asks about a Liberty that is not its own: "their" is not met.
#ASSERT `Does my community keep this Liberty or Custom` `a Town` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- Another Port asks about its own free Custom: kept.
#ASSERT `Does my community keep this Liberty or Custom` `another Port` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`

§§ `Chapter 29: something is done, or threatened, to a Freeman`

-- Arguments: I am a Freeman; what is done to me; there was a lawful judgment of my Peers; it is done by the Law of the Land.
-- A Freeman is imprisoned, with no judgment and no law behind it: forbidden.
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` TRUE `imprisoned` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `forbidden by chapter 29`
-- The same Freeman, after a lawful judgment of his Peers: the chapter does not forbid it.
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` TRUE `imprisoned` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not forbidden by chapter 29`
-- A Freeman is disseised of his Freehold by the Law of the Land: the chapter does not forbid it.
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` TRUE `disseised of his Freehold` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not forbidden by chapter 29`
-- A Freeman is outlawed with neither: forbidden. A Freeman is exiled with neither: forbidden.
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` TRUE `outlawed` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `forbidden by chapter 29`
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` TRUE `exiled` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `forbidden by chapter 29`
-- The King would condemn a Freeman with neither: forbidden ("nor will We ... condemn him, but by ...").
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` TRUE `condemned` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `forbidden by chapter 29`
-- Someone who is not a Freeman is imprisoned: the sentence speaks of a Freeman, so the text is silent.
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` FALSE `imprisoned` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by chapter 29`
-- A Freeman is fined (some other treatment): chapter 29 lists what it lists.
#ASSERT `May I be taken, imprisoned or condemned other than by lawful judgment of my Peers or the Law of the Land` TRUE `some other treatment` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by chapter 29`
-- Who does it? Only "passed upon" and "condemned" are done by We; "imprisoned" names no one.
#ASSERT `Does chapter 29 say who would do it to me` `condemned` EQUALS JUST `the King`
#ASSERT `Does chapter 29 say who would do it to me` `imprisoned` EQUALS NOTHING
-- Arguments: I am a man in the text's sense; what is at stake.
#ASSERT `May Justice or Right be sold to me, denied me or deferred to me` TRUE Justice EQUALS `forbidden by chapter 29`
#ASSERT `May Justice or Right be sold to me, denied me or deferred to me` TRUE Right EQUALS `forbidden by chapter 29`
#ASSERT `May Justice or Right be sold to me, denied me or deferred to me` TRUE `another thing` EQUALS `not covered by chapter 29`
#ASSERT `May Justice or Right be sold to me, denied me or deferred to me` FALSE Justice EQUALS `not covered by chapter 29`

§§ `The General Saving: reserved, observed, promised, of no force, confirmed`

-- Arguments: it is free; I have had it in time passed.
#ASSERT `Are my free Liberties and free Customs reserved to me` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Are my free Liberties and free Customs reserved to me` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- A Templar is named in the reservation's list; a Freeman is reached only as one of "all Persons".
#ASSERT `How does the reservation reach me` `a Templar` EQUALS `named in the list`
#ASSERT `How does the reservation reach me` `a Freeman of the Realm` EQUALS `reached as one of all Persons`
-- Arguments: I am a Man of this our Realm; observing lies within my power.
#ASSERT `Must I observe these Customs and Liberties` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Must I observe these Customs and Liberties` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Must I observe these Customs and Liberties` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- Arguments: it is one of the Customs and Liberties granted; it appertains to the King and his Heirs.
#ASSERT `Is the King bound to observe this Custom or Liberty` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Is the King bound to observe this Custom or Liberty` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- The promise was granted unto those who gave: a Knight is one, a Hospitaller is not named.
#ASSERT `Was the King's promise not to infringe the Liberties granted to me` `a Knight` EQUALS `covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Was the King's promise not to infringe the Liberties granted to me` `a Hospitaller` EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- Arguments: the one who would do it; it infringes or breaks the Liberties.
#ASSERT `May the King or his Heir procure or do a thing that infringes the Liberties` `the King` TRUE EQUALS `forbidden by the text`
#ASSERT `May the King or his Heir procure or do a thing that infringes the Liberties` `an Heir of the King` TRUE EQUALS `forbidden by the text`
#ASSERT `May the King or his Heir procure or do a thing that infringes the Liberties` `the King` FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
#ASSERT `May the King or his Heir procure or do a thing that infringes the Liberties` `a Man of the Realm` TRUE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- Arguments: it was procured by any person; it is contrary to the Liberties and the promises.
#ASSERT `Is a thing procured contrary to these Liberties of any force or effect` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `of no force nor effect`
-- RECONCILED 2026-10-30: was `#ASSERT `Is a thing procured contrary to these Liberties of any force or effect` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the first unnumbered paragraph``
-- Procured by someone, but not contrary to the premises: the "if" fails, so the text does not make it void.
#ASSERT `Is a thing procured contrary to these Liberties of any force or effect` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not made void by the first unnumbered paragraph`
-- The second paragraph.
#ASSERT `Must the Charter and its Articles be observed for ever` TRUE EQUALS `covered by the text`
#ASSERT `Must the Charter and its Articles be observed for ever` FALSE EQUALS `not covered by the text`
-- Arguments: it is an Article of the same Charter; it hath hitherto been kept.
#ASSERT `Does the second paragraph command that an Article not yet kept be observed from henceforth` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `commanded to be firmly observed from henceforth`
#ASSERT `Does the second paragraph command that an Article not yet kept be observed from henceforth` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not commanded by the final clause`
#ASSERT `Has the King confirmed, made strong and renewed this gift or grant` `the grant of the Liberties to the Freemen of chapter 1` EQUALS `confirmed and made strong for the King and his Heirs perpetually, and renewed`
#ASSERT `Has the King confirmed, made strong and renewed this gift or grant` `a gift or grant this text does not show` EQUALS `not covered by the second unnumbered paragraph`

§§ `Layer 1 called directly: the subsidy, which has no seat question`

-- A Baron whose Moveables are worth 4500, in whatever unit he uses: the Fifteenth Part is 300. A Baron is one of the classes who gave.
#ASSERT `first paragraph: the Fifteenth Part of Moveables was given by` `a Baron` EQUALS `covered by the text`
#ASSERT `first paragraph: the Fifteenth Part of` 4500 EQUALS 300
-- A Templar is not among those named as having given: the text is silent about him.
#ASSERT `first paragraph: the Fifteenth Part of Moveables was given by` `a Templar` EQUALS `not covered by the text`
